TI  - A transferable spatiotemporal deep learning framework integrating soil moisture dynamics for high-resolution cropland soil organic matter mapping across regions
PY  - 2026
T2  - Remote Sensing of Environment
VL  - 338
C7  - 115365
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2026.115365
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034479914?origin=resultslist
AB  - Soil organic matter (SOM) is a critical indicator of soil health and agricultural sustainability, yet achieving high-resolution mapping at large scales remains challenging due to the complex interplay of environmental drivers, particularly soil moisture dynamics. This study introduces a novel spatiotemporal deep learning framework, ExP-C, that incorporates refactored soil moisture dynamic feedback sequences (SMDFS) to enhance SOM prediction accuracy. Focusing on two major black soil regions—Northeast China (CN, 1.24 million km2) and the U.S. Mississippi River Basin (USA, 1.35 million km2)—we compiled 2616 topsoil (0-20 cm) samples from field surveys (2014-2024) and the WoSIS database, integrated with Landsat-8 OLI bare-soil composites, SRTM-derived topographic variables, and ERA5 meteorological data. SMDFS are generated via percentile refactoring of multi-temporal moisture indices (MNDWI, NDWI, NDMI) to capture dynamic feedbacks between SOM levels and moisture retention. The ExP-C model fuses expert convolutional networks for spatial contextual extraction from static covariates, pyramid 1D convolutions for multi-scale SMDFS pattern mining, and contrastive learning to bolster feature representations, with transfer learning (pre-training on CN data, fine-tuning on USA data) enabling cross-regional adaptation. Ten-fold cross-validation yielded superior performance for ExP-C compared to random forest and CNN-LSTM baselines: R2 = 0.712 (RMSE = 6.221 g/kg) in CN and R2 = 0.554 (RMSE = 7.693 g/kg) in the USA when integrating SMDFS with spatial features, with SMDFS alone boosting R2 by 13%–17% on static inputs. Gradient-based attribution confirmed SMDFS and elevation as dominant contributors, underscoring their mechanistic role in SOM variability. Transfer learning further improved USA predictions to R2 = 0.572 (RMSE = 7.597 g/kg) using a partial freeze strategy on spatial branches. High-resolution (30 m) SOM maps revealed north-south gradients in both regions, with mean contents of 32.0 g/kg (CN) and 36.8 g/kg (USA), and low uncertainty (means of 4.03 and 4.79 g/kg, respectively). This framework advances SOM mapping by explicitly modeling moisture-SOM feedbacks, offering a robust, transferable approach for monitoring carbon dynamics in global croplands and informing sustainable management. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
