AB  - Accurate estimation of subsurface electrical properties is essential for a wide range of applications, including geophysical exploration and precision agriculture. These properties - particularly permittivity and conductivity - are critical for evaluating parameters such as soil moisture, which plays a key role in agricultural water resource management. Several conventional techniques have been developed, including Time-Domain Reflectometry (TDR) and Frequency-Domain Reflectometry (FDR), which require physical sensor deployment, and satellite-based methods, which offer broader coverage but suffer from poor spatial resolution and shallow penetration depth. Ground-penetrating radar (GPR), as a non-invasive technique, offers an effective alternative by capturing reflections of electromagnetic waves to infer subsurface structures and material properties [1]. Although conventional and GPRbased methods have improved over time, they continue to face challenges such as limited generalization, sensitivity to environmental variations, and the complexity of modeling. These limitations motivate the development of alternative datadriven approaches.  © 2025 IEEE.
