TI  - Assessment of Sentinel-1 Satellite Imagery for Soybean and Potato Crop Classification for Dharwad, During Kharif Crop Cycles
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Next-Gen Technologies of Artificial Intelligence and Geoscience Remote Sensing, EarthSense 2025
DO  - 10.1109/EarthSense66084.2025.11297348
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032190739?origin=resultslist
AB  - Accurate and timely crop classification is critical for modern agricultural monitoring. Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) offers a powerful, all-weather alternative, effectively distinguishing objects based on shape, orientation, and size. However, classifying crops like potato and soybean remains challenging due to their similar leaf area and shapes, especially in regions with concurrent planting schedules. Initial analysis of Sentinel-1 SAR data (VV, VH backscatter, VH/VV ratio) and optical-derived Leaf Area Index (LAI) revealed similar temporal profiles and peak values for both crops (e.g., August peak VH: -14.41 dB vs. -14.19 dB; VV: -7.66 dB vs. -7.93 dB; LAI: 21.75 vs. 22), limiting differentiation based solely on backscatter. This study investigates the synergistic integration of soil moisture and Gray Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM)-derived textural features from SAR data to improve potato and soybean classification. We hypothesize these parameters, by capturing spatial variations in crop structural and phenological attributes, will enhance accuracy. Employing Random Forest (RF) and Support Vector Machine (SVM) algorithms, our results demonstrate that the fusion of SAR backscatter, soil moisture, and GLCM texture features significantly improves classification precision, offering valuable insights for precision agriculture.  © 2025 IEEE.
