TI  - A Spatial Downscaling Method for Remote Sensing Soil Moisture Using Adaptive Weighted Stacking Strategy
PY  - 2025
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Geoscience and Remote Sensing
VL  - 63
C7  - 4421212
DO  - 10.1109/TGRS.2025.3626415
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020437013?origin=resultslist
AB  - Soil moisture (SM) derived from remote sensing plays a crucial role in understanding land-atmosphere interactions between water and carbon cycles. However, existing remotely sensed surface SM products (e.g., European Space Agency (ESA) Climate Change Initiative (CCI) SM) have relatively coarse spatial resolutions (25–40 km), limiting their suitability for precision agriculture and ecological management. To address this limitation, this study proposes an Adaptive Weighted Stacking Strategy (AWSS) for SM downscaling. A stacking framework integrating random forest (RF), gradient-boosted regression trees (GBRT), and extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) was developed to downscale 25-km resolution ESA CCI SM data to a high-resolution 1-km product. Key predictors, including surface albedo, apparent thermal inertia (ATI), clay content, and leaf area index (LAI), were identified through Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) feature importance analysis. An adaptive weight strategy was then introduced to dynamically optimize the contributions of each base model. The downscaled SM was validated using in situ SM measurements from the Murrumbidgee River Basin. Results indicate that both GBRT ( R =0.916 and root mean square error (RMSE) = 0.046 m3/m3) and XGBoost ( R = 0.915 and RMSE = 0.047 m3/m3) models significantly outperformed the RF model ( R =0.847 and RMSE = 0.066 m3/m3). Notably, the stacking strategy method that combines a linear regression meta model with adaptive weighting achieved the best performance ( R = 0.931 and RMSE = 0.041 m3/m3). The downscaled SM exhibits finer spatial details of within-field variability compared with the original CCI SM. Further spatiotemporal analysis confirmed that the downloaded SM effectively captures precipitation response and seasonal variations, particularly providing a more detailed representation in farmland and pastureland regions. This study provides an effective method for high-resolution SM monitoring in semi-arid areas, with significant applications in agricultural irrigation, water resource management, and climate change research. © 1980-2012 IEEE.
