AB  - Soil moisture is a critical component of the Earth’s hydrological cycle, influencing agricultural productivity, ecological stability, and climate regulation. This chapter explores the significance of soil moisture, its impact on plant health and weather patterns, and the importance of its proper management in agriculture. Various methods for measuring soil moisture, including gravimetric techniques, tensiometers, time domain reflectometry (TDR), capacitance sensors, neutron moisture gauges, and remote sensing technologies, are detailed. The chapter discusses the application of remote sensing in agriculture, highlighting how advanced agrogeophysical methods such as electrical conductivity (EC), electromagnetic (EM) induction, and ground-penetrating radar (GPR) provide comprehensive insights into soil properties, enhancing soil management strategies. These techniques offer valuable data for precision irrigation, crop monitoring, yield prediction, risk management, and environmental conservation, driving improvements in agricultural efficiency and sustainability. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Mukesh Gupta; individual chapters, the contributors.
