AB  - Precision Agriculture (PA) is a transformative approach in modern farming that leverages advanced technologies such as remote sensing, GPS, and the Internet of Things (IoT) to enhance resource efficiency and crop yields. Water efficiency, a critical component of PA, addresses the global challenge of water scarcity in agriculture by enabling farmers to apply water only where and when it is needed. Variable Rate Irrigation (VRI) systems can reduce water usage while maintaining crop yields. Precision irrigation combined with soil moisture sensors can improve water use productivity by up to 30%, allowing farmers to optimize irrigation schedules in real time. This precision results in lower energy consumption and operational costs, as decision support systems help reduce water and energy use in irrigation management. PA technologies also enable site-specific management, allowing proper watering of crops according to spatial water heterogeneity within fields, reducing over-irrigation, waterlogging, and nutrient leaching. Overall, Precision Agriculture offers a viable strategy to improve water use efficiency in agriculture while promoting sustainable crop production through sophisticated tools and methods. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Soufiane Haddout and Tonni Agustiono Kurniawan; individual chapters, the contributors.
