AB  - As a critical geophysical parameter governing land-atmosphere interactions, soil moisture (SM) plays a pivotal role inregulating global water and energy cycles. Despite the proliferationof satellite-derived SM products, their utility in precision agricul-ture remains constrained by inherent limitations in spatial resolu-tion and cross-regional generalizability. This study presents a novelmachine learning framework that synergistically integrates mul-tisource earth observation data with in-situ measurements fromthe International Soil Moisture Network (ISMN) to address theselimitations. Leveraging the cloud-based computational capabilitiesof Google Earth Engine (GEE), we developed a high-resolution(60m) SM estimation model through systematically synergistic in-tegration of Sentinel-2 multispectral reflectance, Sentinel-1 C-bandSAR backscatter characteristics (VV, VH polarization, incidenceangle) and ISMN ground measurements. The microwave-canopyinteraction mechanisms were quantitatively characterized usingthe Michigan Microwave Canopy Scattering (MIMICS) model, in-forming the selection of optimal predictor variables from Sentinel-2MSI spectral bands (B1-B8A, B9-B12) and SoilGrids texture pa-rameters. A streamlined machine learning regression architecturewas implemented, achieving optimized performance through fea-ture importance analysis that identified clay content (32.98%) andsand content (28.25%) as dominant predictors. Validation acrossheterogeneous agricultural regions demonstrated robust perfor-mance (Bias < 0.05 cm3 /cm3 ) in low-to-moderate moisture regimes(< 0.36 cm 3 /cm 3 ), with systematic underestimation observed insaturated conditions. The operational pipeline generated monthlycomposite SM maps for Henan Province croplands (April 2018), es-tablishing a transferable paradigm for global-scale high-resolutionSM monitoring, particularly in low-stature crop environments (e.g.,winter wheat). This methodology advances the synergistic use ofmicrowave-optical-soil property data fusion and provides criticalinsights for extending the framework to other vegetation types. © 2008-2012 IEEE.
