AB  - Monitoring the performance of crops is fundamental for agricultural planning, food security and resource management. To address this, the present study introduces a novel hybrid deep learningmodelling approach that combines remote sensing measurements of satellite imageryby the Sentinel-2 mission and in-situ soil moisture, temperature, and nutrientlevels. A convolutionalneural network is used for extracting spatial features and a long short-term memory network is utilized for modeling the temporal patterns from the sensor streams. The composite model forecasts yield at the field level for several primary crops cultivated in Punjab, India. This model with an R2 of 0.92 and a RMSE of 3.8 quintals/hectare is better than the CNN-only and LSTM-only baselines. It performs consistently across crop types and growth stages, indicating that it is robust to regional variation. This methodology allows for data driven yield forecasting, applicable to precision agriculture but also to government planning, crop insurance, farm-level decisions, etc. Further developments will consider the use of Sentinel-1SAR imagery and other agronomic variables for enhancement of scalability and resilience in cloudier conditions. © 2025 IEEE.
