SP  - 257
EP  - 273
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-12770-9_12
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032068218?origin=resultslist
AB  - Smart irrigation systems, integrating sensors, IoT, and AI, address global water scarcity affecting 2.4 × 109 people and support food security for a projected 9.7 × 109 population by 2050. Achieving 80–95% water use efficiency compared to 40–70% for traditional methods, these systems reduce water waste by 20–50%, enhance crop yields by 10–20%, and cut energy use by 10–20%. This chapter explores the need for smart irrigation amid rising agricultural water demands, detailing core technologies like soil moisture sensors (±2–5% accuracy), automated drip systems, and predictive analytics. Implementation strategies, case studies from California vineyards and Kenyan smallholder farms, and adoption challenges, including high costs (USD 1200–3500/ha) and rural connectivity issues, are analyzed. Benefits include reduced runoff (50–60%) and lower carbon emissions (5–7% of agriculture’s 1.2 × 1010 kg CO₂e/year). Future trends highlight affordable sensors (USD 20–50 by 2030), solar integration, and satellite imagery, with scalability to 30% of 3.0 × 108 ha irrigated land, saving 1.2 × 1011 m3 water annually. Practical steps and policy incentives are proposed to ensure sustainable agriculture, fostering a water-secure future. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
