TI  - Relative surface evapotranspiration index (RSETI): A novel approach for drought characterization in Australia
PY  - 2025
T2  - Remote Sensing of Environment
VL  - 329
C7  - 114948
DO  - 10.1016/j.rse.2025.114948
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105012397381?origin=resultslist
AB  - Drought is a recurrent and severe hazard in Australia, exerting substantial pressure on ecosystems and human communities. Accurate drought assessment, reliant on precise estimates of evapotranspiration (ET) and soil moisture (SM), is critical for effective water resource management and irrigation planning. This study introduces the Relative Surface ET Index (RSETI), a novel drought indicator derived from the Normalized Difference Latent Heat Index (NDLI) using green, red, and short-wave infrared (SWIR) 1 bands. The drought-monitoring capabilities of RSETI, alongside existing indices, were evaluated using diverse datasets, such as downscaled Global Land Surface Satellite (GLASS) SM, Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) ET and Net Primary Productivity (NPP), flux tower data (ET, Gross Primary Productivity [GPP], and SM), Soil Moisture Active Passive (SMAP) surface and root-zone SM, and Standardized Precipitation-ET Index (SPEI). Across Australia, the RSETI exhibited the highest correlation (r) with GLASS SM (0.78 ≤ r ≤ 0.82, p < 0.01) and showed greater sensitivity than other indices. The Temperature-Vegetation-Precipitation Dryness Index (TVPDI) indicated strong correlations with MODIS ET (r = 0.91) and NPP (r = 0.92), while the RSETI excelled in indicating ET and NPP, achieving highest r values of 0.94 and 0.91, respectively. Spatiotemporal correlations revealed strong consistency between the RSETI and datasets, namely SMAP Surface SM, MODIS ET and NPP, Vegetation Health Index (VHI), Temperature-Vegetation-SWIR Reflectance Dryness Index (TVSDI), and TVPDI, covering the majority of Australia. Validations using flux tower data showed that the RSETI outperformed TVPDI and TVSDI at most sites. Using Random Forest regression, monthly RSETI-based ET estimates for 2017–2019 showed strong agreement with MODIS ET (0.61 ≤ r ≤ 0.95), indicating its capability for ET monitoring. Given its capability to represent ET and SM, the RSETI was used to assess drought conditions in Australia from 2001 to 2022. The results identified severe drought events during the Millennium Drought (2001–2009) and Tinderbox Drought (2017–2019), highly affecting vegetation and water availability. Owing to its simplicity and broad applicability, the RSETI holds high potential for drought monitoring and offers valuable support for resilience planning and sustainable agriculture. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.
