TI  - Effective Remote-Controlled Irrigation with Arduino and LoRa for Sustainable Agriculture
PY  - 2025
T2  - Conference Proceedings - 2025 IEEE Silchar Subsection Conference, IEEE SILCON 2025
DO  - 10.1109/SILCON67893.2025.11327375
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034984530?origin=resultslist
AB  - Current agriculture suffers from ongoing problems like water deficiency, wasteful irrigation, and continuous human monitoring. Solutions to these must be intelligent and automatic but available and sustainable as well. This article discusses the creation of an automatic irrigation system based on LoRa, utilizing Arduino microcontrollers and environmental sensors to enable remote and intelligent farm management. With cost effectiveness, scalability and energy savings in mind, the system allows farmers to monitor and regulate irrigation in real-time as a function of soil moisture and atmospheric conditions. Hardware architecture consists of two major modules: a field-installed transmitter unit and a receiver unit based at the farmer's house or control room. The transmitter has a DHT11 temperature-humidity sensor and a soil moisture sensor connected to an Arduino Uno, which receives and transmits environmental data through the LoRa SX1278 module. At the receiving station, another Arduino Uno decodes the data received and compares it to preset thresholds. If the soil moisture drops below the specified level, the system triggers a relay to initiate a water pump; when a sufficient moisture level is sensed, the pump is automatically switched off, thereby avoiding water wastage and over-irrigation. The system is powered on a reliable 5 V DC supply which is obtained from a 230 V AC source using 7805 voltage regulators. An LCD module gives real-time feedback of environmental factors and system operations.  © 2025 IEEE.
