DO  - 10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.02.038
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033015074?origin=resultslist
AB  - Vegetation greenness forecasting is essential for farmers and land managers, enabling them to proactively implement crucial agronomic practices such as irrigation, fertilization, crop rotation, and pest control, based on expected future conditions. However, successful frameworks for forecasting vegetation greenness, especially on a global scale, are currently lacking. This study proposes a spatiotemporal attention model designed to adaptively select both regions and key frames for forecasting global monthly vegetation greenness. Results demonstrated that the highest predictability of greenness with less than 10% error was found in areas with dense tree cover and long growing seasons, while regions with extensive cropland, grassland, shorter growing seasons, or high latitudes exhibited lower predictability. Soil moisture, temperature and forest cover percentage are the three most important factors in forecasting global vegetation greenness, accounting for 38.5%, 32.1%, and 10.9% of the global area, respectively. The area weighted global mean greenness forecasting error for 1-, 3-, and 6-month forecast is 14.93%, 15.36%, and 15.54%, respectively. These forecasts enable better planning for forest management, sustainable agriculture, carbon sequestration, and the prevention of land degradation, highlighting their significance for both local and global environmental sustainability efforts and food security. © 2026 International Society for Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing, Inc. (ISPRS). Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
