TI  - Robust gradient boosting tree model for soil moisture prediction based on Huber loss and residual weighting using optical sensing fusion
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of SPIE - The International Society for Optical Engineering
VL  - 14064
C7  - 1406454
DO  - 10.1117/12.3094263
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030487458?origin=resultslist
AB  - The goal of this paper is to enhance intelligent perception capabilities in precision agriculture. Given that environmental data collected from both meteorological instruments and optical sensing systems (e.g., remote sensing imagery, multispectral reflectance sensors) often suffer from instability, outliers, and cross-modality disturbances, traditional regression models generally struggle with robustness, sensitivity to noise, and heavy dependence on manual parameter tuning. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a soil moisture prediction method, Robust Weighted Gradient Boosting Tree (RW-GBT), which integrates robust modeling with an adaptive multi-objective optimization mechanism. Within the GBDT framework, the model incorporates the Huber loss function to improve anomaly resistance, introduces a residual weighting mechanism to prioritize learning on difficult samples, and adopts a Bayesian optimization strategy to jointly optimize for prediction accuracy and stability under disturbances. Experimental validation on a real-world dataset combining meteorological and optical sensing data demonstrates that RW-GBT outperforms baseline models (e.g., extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), support vector regression (SVR), random forests (RF)) in both RMSE and robustness to synthetic noise and optical perturbations. Ablation experiments confirm the independent contributions of each submodule, while hyperparameter sensitivity analysis and time-series visualization highlight the model's generalization under sensor drift and input fluctuation. In conclusion, RW-GBT provides an efficient and scalable solution for robust soil moisture prediction in noisy, multi-modal environments, with broad applicability in smart agriculture, optical remote sensing integration, and environmental monitoring systems.  © COPYRIGHT SPIE. Downloading of the abstract is permitted for personal use only.
