UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034151566?origin=resultslist
AB  - The integration of unmanned aerial systems (UAS) with the Portable L-band Radiometer (PoLRa) offers a flexible and effective approach for mapping high-resolution soil moisture within the top 5 cm of soil. This capability holds significant potential for applications such as precision agriculture and urban environmental monitoring. However, most evaluations of the UAS-mounted L-band radiometers for soil moisture retrievals have focused on areas with homogeneous land cover, such agricultural fields. This study expands the scope by assessing soil moisture retrievals from a UAS-based L-band radiometer across landscapes with abrupt transitions, ranging from forests, gradient grasslands, and bare soil. By comparing the PoLRa-derived soil moisture retrievals – at spatial resolutions of 10 m, 20 m, and 30 m – with benchmarks measured by a handheld probe, we observed an average unbiased root-mean-square error below 0.04 m3/m3 and a positive bias of approximately 0.07 m3/m3. While the UAS-based L-band radiometer effectively captures spatial patterns of surface wetness associated with different land cover types, it struggled to resolve variations within forested areas. Future work should prioritize refining calibration procedures for observed brightness temperatures and systematically incorporating ancillary data to further enhance soil moisture retrieval accuracy from UAS-based L-band radiometers. © 2025 IEEE.
