TI  - Assessing the Impact of Vegetated Land Cover on Downscaling Satellite Soil Moisture Products
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Geoscience and Remote Sensing Symposium (IGARSS)  
SP  - 733
EP  - 737
DO  - 10.1109/IGARSS55030.2025.11243971
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033603253?origin=resultslist
AB  - Soil moisture (SM) is an important hydrological variable in the global water and energy cycle. Timely and accurate SM information at high spatial resolution is vital for agriculture, water management, and other hydro-climatic applications. This study aims to enhance the spatial resolution of satellite-derived SM products, specifically the Soil Moisture Active Passive-Enhanced (SMAP-E) 9 km product, for the Yanco region in the Murrumbidgee River catchment, Australia. The downscaling approach developed in this study is based on soil thermal inertia theory and employs a random forest (RF) algorithm that considers seasonal variability. In-situ SM data were related to near-surface diurnal temperature differences, daily median temperature, normalised difference vegetation index (NDVI), and clay content. To downscale the SMAP-E data to a 1 km spatial resolution, Moderate-resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) land surface temperature, NDVI and clay content datasets were used. The results were validated using ground-based monitoring and airborne SM data. The downscaled SM products showed a root mean square error (RMSE) ranging from 0.06-0.15 cm3/cm3 compared to airborne SM. Areas with high bias were analysed in relation to land use maps and Sentinel-1 mean, standard deviation, and cross ratio (CR) derived from VV, VH backscatter, to account for the influence of land surface characteristics. Including Sentinel-1 parameters resulted in a modest improvement in the RF model’s accuracy, despite the limited training data and lack of seasonal adjustments. CR indicated a strong capability for representing the spatiotemporal variability of vegetated land cover. This study underscores the importance of considering land cover variability in downscaling satellite-based SM products for effective hydrological and agricultural applications. © 2025 IEEE.
