TI  - Role of remote sensing in assessing total water storage and evapotranspiration for croplands in the mekong River Basin
PY  - 2022
T2  - Remote Sensing of Agriculture and Land Cover/Land Use Changes in South and Southeast Asian Countries
SP  - 333
EP  - 349
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-030-92365-5_19
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85154592787?origin=resultslist
AB  - The Mekong river basin is a major agricultural area with irrigated and rainfed croplands covering 44% of the total area. Assessing the soil moisture storage using remote sensing data and biophysically-based hydrological model estimates of the water budget components can serve as a useful method for seasonal and annual water resources management and decision-making. In this study, we compute the terrestrial water storage change (TWSC) and anomaly (TWSA) based on variable infiltration capacity model (VIC) simulation with a) irrigation b) without irrigation, and c) MODIS-derived evapotranspiration (ET). TWSC derived from these three methods were compared against the GRACE data. While the ET derived by MODIS compared well with VIC-irrigation simulation, both TWSC and TWSA have seasonal and annual scale differences. Overall, the irrigated regions showed good agreement; however, biases in the rainfed regions of the basin and the delta region suggest the deficiencies in realistically simulating the water budget components. We conclude that even though spatial and temporal signatures are broadly captured, land use classification, soil parameters, and representation of groundwater influences can vastly improve the correlation between hydrological estimates and remote sensing data. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022. All rights reserved.
