AU  - Tsakiridis, N.L.
TI  - Estimation of soil properties for sustainable crop production using multisource data fusion
PY  - 2024
T2  - Hyperautomation in Precision Agriculture: Advancements and Opportunities for Sustainable Farming
SP  - 113
EP  - 127
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-443-24139-0.00010-2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85213172209?origin=resultslist
AB  - This chapter explores the potential of multisource data fusion for estimating soil properties and its implications for sustainable crop production. Advanced technologies, including in situ measurements from MEMS and full-range spectrometers, pH or soil moisture sensors, as well as airborne and space-borne data encompassing radar, multispectral data, and hyperspectral data, provide a plethora of information sources for comprehensive soil physicochemical analysis (e.g., texture, organic matter, nutrients, etc.). Additionally, the emerging trend of crowdsourcing data and widespread usage of mobile devices presents novel opportunities; such user-generated data, when combined with traditional sources, enhances spatial coverage and the richness of soil information. Data fusion offers a promising approach to improving accuracy and efficiency by combining different data types, enabling a holistic understanding of soil characteristics. Various methodologies for data fusion, including statistical modeling, machine learning, and geospatial techniques, are discussed. Challenges such as data heterogeneity, variability, and scale disparities are addressed with proposed strategies. Practical applications of multisource data fusion support sustainable crop production by optimizing fertilization, irrigation, and management practices, leading to increased productivity, resource efficiency, and reduced environmental impact. This chapter provides an in-depth exploration of in situ, airborne, and space-borne data integration, showcasing the potential of multisource data fusion in advancing soil management and crop production. The insights presented serve as a foundation for researchers, practitioners, and policymakers seeking to harness diverse data sources for improved agriculture. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
